  // We added this to make sure that "node" knows that we are not using the newer EJS syntax.
  // What was the need to specify this explicitly ? 
    // Node supports two incompatibly syntaxs that is they cannot be interchange
    // if we would not have specified it the 'node' parser would have assume that is the 'CommonJS'(the other syntax)
    // but would have encountered keywords like export or import and thrown us a warning.
  // the alternative solution would have been to add .mjs as extension to whichever file has the new syntax style (EJS)
  // and .js to the files that uses the older syntax style 'Common js' ( but that would have been a hack or like a workaround)
  "type": "module" 
